  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Given an integer NNN, construct an array AAA of length NNN such that:\n\n\n1≤Ai≤10181 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^{18}1≤Ai​≤1018 for all (1≤i≤N)(1 \\le i \\le N)(1≤i≤N);\nThere exists a subsequence of length **greater** than 111 such that the gcd of all elements of the subsequence is iii, for all 1≤i≤N1 \\le i \\le N1≤i≤N.\n\nMore formally, for all (1≤i≤N)(1 \\leq i \\leq N)(1≤i≤N), there exists a subsequence SSS of array AAA such that the length of the subsequence is k(2≤k≤N)k (2 \\leq k \\leq N)k(2≤k≤N) and gcd(S1,S2,…,Sk)=igcd(S_1,S_2,\\ldots,S_k)=igcd(S1​,S2​,…,Sk​)=i.\nIt can be proven that it is always possible to construct such AAA under given constraints. If there exist multiple such arrays, print any.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input contains a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follow.\nThe only line of each test case contains an integer NNN - the length of the array to be constructed.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output a single line containing NNN space-separated integers, denoting the elements of the array AAA. The ithi^{th}ith of these NNN integers is the ithi^{th}ith element of the array AAA.\n\n\nIf there exist multiple such arrays, print any.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1031 \\leq T \\leq 10^31≤T≤103",
      "3≤N≤1033 \\leq N \\leq 10^33≤N≤103",
      "Sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 5⋅1035 \\cdot 10^35⋅103."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n3\n4",
        "output": "2 3 6\n4 24 10 15",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: A possible array satisfying all the conditions is [2, 3, 6]:\n\nFor i=1i=1i=1: Choose S=[A1,A2,A3]=[2,3,6]S = [A_1, A_2, A_3] = [2, 3, 6]S=[A1​,A2​,A3​]=[2,3,6]. Thus, gcd(2,3,6)=1gcd(2,3,6)=1gcd(2,3,6)=1.\nFor i=2i=2i=2: Choose S=[A1,A3]=[2,6]S = [A_1, A_3] = [2, 6]S=[A1​,A3​]=[2,6]. Thus, gcd(2,6)=2gcd(2,6)=2gcd(2,6)=2.\nFor i=3i=3i=3: Choose S=[A2,A3]=[3,6]S = [A_2, A_3] = [3, 6]S=[A2​,A3​]=[3,6]. Thus, gcd(3,6)=3gcd(3,6)=3gcd(3,6)=3.\n\nTest case 222: A possible array satisfying all the conditions is [4, 24, 10, 15]:\n\nFor i=1i=1i=1: Choose S=[4,24,10,15]S = [4, 24, 10, 15]S=[4,24,10,15]. Thus, gcd(4,24,10,15)=1gcd(4,24,10,15)=1gcd(4,24,10,15)=1.\nFor i=2i=2i=2: Choose S=[24,10]S = [24, 10]S=[24,10]. Thus, gcd(24,10)=2gcd(24,10)=2gcd(24,10)=2.\nFor i=3i=3i=3: Choose S=[24,15]S = [24, 15]S=[24,15]. Thus, gcd(24,15)=3gcd(24,15)=3gcd(24,15)=3.\nFor i=4i=4i=4: Choose S=[4,24]S = [4, 24]S=[4,24]. Thus, gcd(4,24)=4gcd(4,24)=4gcd(4,24)=4."
      }
    ]
  },
